//! Declarative rigid-body physics for the optional `physics` hook.
//!
//! Shapes and bodies are values, body attributes pipe naturally, and stable
//! branded tags connect declarations, reads, commands, and collision events.
//!
//! The hook DECLARES the world each frame and the runtime reconciles that
//! declaration against the live one. A tag is cross-frame identity: the same
//! tag is the same body, and a body is dropped by no longer declaring it.
//! Re-declaring an unchanged body leaves the simulation alone, while changing
//! its declared position or rotation drives that field — a dynamic or fixed
//! body teleports immediately, and a kinematic body takes the new pose as its
//! next-step target, so it carries velocity into contacts. Reading a tag that
//! is not in the live world is a runtime error rather than an empty answer, so
//! read only bodies your hook has declared. Like the model, the world survives
//! hot reload for as long as the hook does; deleting the hook drops it. An
//! error raised inside the hook does NOT stop the world: the previous frame's
//! declaration is kept and stepped, and the error is reported once.
//!
//! Reads are SYNCHRONOUS and writes are QUEUED. `Physics.position`,
//! `Physics.linearVelocity`, and `Physics.cast` answer in place from the LAST
//! STEPPED world — in any entry point, the `physics` hook included. Everything
//! that runs before the step (`tick`, `input`, a pre-step `update`, the hook)
//! sees the previous step; only `draw` and the post-step `update`s see the
//! world this frame just stepped. The world is primed from `init` before the
//! first frame, so frame 1's reads answer with the initial declared poses.
//! Every mutation instead returns an `Effect.t` that applies at the next
//! physics step after it queues — after reconcile, on that step's first
//! substep. A command issued from `tick` therefore normally lands in time for
//! the same frame's `draw`; on a frame that takes no substep at all (normal
//! above 60fps) it waits for the next simulated frame.

/// An opaque collision shape.
type shape = host
/// An opaque rigid body description.
type body = host
/// An opaque physics world description.
type world = host
/// A stable, branded body identity used throughout the physics API.
type tag

/// The live world-space position of a body.
type position = { x: float, y: float, z: float }
/// The live linear velocity of a body, in world units per second.
type velocity = { x: float, y: float, z: float }
/// A raycast result with hit position, normal, distance, and body tag.
///
/// `triangle` is the hit triangle's index in the `Physics.trimesh` data, or
/// -1 off a trimesh. For a miss, `hit` is false, `triangle` is -1 and the
/// remaining fields are zeroed.
type rayHit = {
  hit: bool,
  x: float, y: float, z: float,
  nx: float, ny: float, nz: float,
  distance: float,
  tag: tag,
  triangle: float
}
/// A contact-begin or contact-end event between two bodies.
type collisionEvent = { started: bool, a: tag, b: tag, sensor: bool }

/// Construct a stable body tag from a string.
///
/// The empty tag is reserved as the no-body sentinel in a raycast miss.
///
/// The brand is check-time only: declare a tag once and use that VALUE at
/// every site — a bare string where a tag is expected is a check error. At
/// runtime a tag simply IS its string, so comparing one against a collision
/// event's `a`/`b` with `==` works.
let tag : (string) => tag

/// Create a local-axis box shape from its full width, height, and depth.
let box : (float, float, float) => shape
/// Create a sphere shape from its radius.
let sphere : (float) => shape
/// Create a capsule shape from half-height and radius.
let capsule : (float, float) => shape

/// Triangle-mesh data for `Physics.trimesh`, in `Model.skinned`'s layout:
/// `positions` is little-endian f32 `x, y, z` per vertex (Y up, metres) and
/// `triangles` is u32 vertex index triples.
type triangles = { positions: Bytes.t, triangles: Bytes.t }

/// Create triangle-mesh collision geometry: level floors, walls and ramps.
///
/// Use it with `Physics.fixed` or `Physics.kinematic`; `Physics.dynamic` is an
/// error. Faces are surfaces, not a solid. Bodies collide with a face's front,
/// the side its triangle winds counter-clockwise, and pass through its back;
/// rays hit both sides. A fast, small body can also pass through a face in one
/// step; continuous collision detection for such bodies is a follow-up.
///
/// Bad data is an error naming the field: an index out of range, a
/// non-finite position, or no triangle with any area. Zero-area triangles
/// are dropped. Build it once (a top-level `let`) and re-declare the value:
/// the collision structure is built once per value, but each call copies and
/// validates the data.
let trimesh : (triangles) => shape

/// Create a fixed heightfield body from a shared terrain descriptor.
///
/// The renderer and collider share dimensions, elevation range, asset
/// resolution, and pending-asset chain. Collision uses at most 1025 samples
/// per axis to bound frame-thread work; larger render sources are decimated.
/// The body supports translation via `Physics.at`; pair it only with an
/// unrotated, unscaled `Scene.terrain`, using the same translation.
let heightfield : (tag, Terrain.t) => body

/// Create a fixed body from the room fused from the environment depth.
///
/// Needs `config`'s `Xr.scene(true)`: the runtime fuses the headset's depth
/// (the stand-in room's on desktop and web) into a mesh, as
/// `Frame.withFusedRoom` draws it, and this body collides with it — the
/// room need not be drawn. Each 64 cm chunk is a static triangle mesh,
/// rebuilt when that chunk re-meshes, so balls bounce off the real walls and
/// furniture as soon as they are seen. The floor — the lowest level surface
/// of at least 0.5 m² at least 1 m below the highest the headset has been —
/// is an infinite plane instead of the fused floor's ripple, so a resting
/// ball stays still and nothing falls through floor nobody has looked at
/// yet. It is found by height below the head, not at `y = 0`: a headset's
/// stage origin is not always on the floor.
///
/// New surfaces reach the body at the physics step after they fuse. The
/// room is already in world space: `Physics.at` and the rotations are
/// errors. Pipe `Physics.friction` and `Physics.restitution` to tune
/// contacts; a ray or contact on any chunk reports this tag. Walls are
/// surfaces with no thickness: give fast, small bodies `Physics.continuous`.
/// Like `Physics.trimesh`, contacts are one-sided: a body behind a wall
/// passes through it (rays still hit both sides).
let fusedRoom : (tag) => body

/// Create a dynamic body affected by forces and collisions.
let dynamic : (tag, shape) => body
/// Create a kinematic body driven explicitly by the game.
let kinematic : (tag, shape) => body
/// Create a fixed body that does not move.
///
/// Still box, sphere and capsule fixed bodies (not sensors) fuse into one
/// collider per material and 16 m cell, so pairs of them cost nothing per
/// step. Changing one rebuilds its cell's collider: a body that moves every
/// frame belongs `Physics.kinematic`.
let fixed : (tag, shape) => body

/// How a `Physics.character` moves. Lengths are metres: the tallest ledge it
/// steps up onto, how far down a grounded move follows the ground (stairs,
/// slopes) instead of stepping off into a fall, and the gap it keeps from the
/// world (positive, about 0.02). It walks slopes up to `maxSlope` and slides
/// down steeper ones.
type characterSettings = { stepHeight: float, maxSlope: Angle.t, snap: float, offset: float }

/// Create a character: a kinematic capsule that `Physics.move` walks through
/// the world, colliding and sliding against every other body except sensors.
///
/// Its position is world state, like any body's: read it with
/// `Physics.position`, and declare it with `Physics.at` once, not every frame
/// (a changed declaration teleports it). Keep the vertical speed in your model:
/// `Physics.grounded` says when to reset it.
let character : (tag, shape, characterSettings) => body

/// Set a body's initial world position; the body is last for piping.
let at : (Vec3.t, body) => body
/// Rotate a body about world X around its center; the body is last for piping.
///
/// An outer rotation applies last in world space, matching `Scene.rotateX`.
/// Heightfield bodies reject rotation because terrain rendering is
/// translation-only.
let rotateX : (Angle.t, body) => body
/// Rotate a body about world Y around its center; the body is last for piping.
///
/// An outer rotation applies last in world space, matching `Scene.rotateY`.
/// Heightfield bodies reject rotation because terrain rendering is
/// translation-only.
let rotateY : (Angle.t, body) => body
/// Rotate a body about world Z around its center; the body is last for piping.
///
/// An outer rotation applies last in world space, matching `Scene.rotateZ`.
/// Heightfield bodies reject rotation because terrain rendering is
/// translation-only.
let rotateZ : (Angle.t, body) => body
/// Set a body's initial linear velocity; the body is last for piping.
let velocity : (Vec3.t, body) => body
/// Set a body's mass; the body is last for piping.
let mass : (float, body) => body
/// Set a body's friction coefficient; the body is last for piping.
let friction : (float, body) => body
/// Set a body's restitution; the body is last for piping.
let restitution : (float, body) => body

/// Damp a body's LINEAR velocity — drag, per second; the body is last for piping.
///
/// The default is `0.0` (no drag), so a rolling sphere on a box coasts almost
/// forever: contact friction resists sliding, not rolling. A small value
/// (`0.3`–`0.8`) is what makes a marble, a puck, or a thrown prop actually
/// settle, and it belongs here rather than in a per-frame velocity command:
/// damping is a property of the body, so declaring it keeps `tick` pure.
/// Must not be negative. Only `dynamic` bodies integrate, so damping is inert
/// on a `kinematic` or `fixed` one. Changing the value writes it onto the live
/// body (the friction/restitution rule) — the new drag applies from the next
/// step, with the body's current pose and velocity untouched.
let linearDamping : (float, body) => body

/// Damp a body's ANGULAR velocity — spin resistance, per second; the body is
/// last for piping.
///
/// The default is `0.0`. Pair it with `linearDamping` for a ball that stops
/// rolling instead of creeping, or use it alone to bleed off spin while linear
/// motion is preserved. Must not be negative, `dynamic`-only, and reconciles
/// onto a live body exactly like `linearDamping`.
let angularDamping : (float, body) => body

/// Make a body a non-solid sensor; the body is last for piping.
let sensor : (body) => body

/// Lock a body's rotation so it translates but never tips.
///
/// The character-controller attribute: an upright capsule that lands, scuffs a
/// ledge, or leans on a wall would otherwise pick up angular velocity and
/// topple, which also invalidates any fixed standing-height assumption a
/// grounding probe makes. The body is last for piping.
let upright : (body) => body

/// Sweep a fast body along each physics step (continuous collision
/// detection), so it cannot pass through a thin surface between two steps;
/// the body is last for piping.
///
/// Without it a body is tested only where each step lands it, and one that
/// moves farther than its own radius per step (a 5 cm ball at a few metres a
/// second, at 60 steps a second) can end a step past a wall that is only a
/// surface, like `Physics.fusedRoom`'s. It costs only while the body is
/// fast. Changing it reconciles onto the live body.
let continuous : (body) => body

/// A box field for `Physics.forceVolume`. `center` and the full `size` are
/// world-axis (it does not rotate). `force` is in newtons, so a light body
/// accelerates more than a heavy one. `turbulence` is the peak gust per axis
/// in newtons (`0.0` is steady): smooth, seeded by the volume's and the
/// body's tags, so a replay gusts identically. `drag` takes that fraction of a
/// body's velocity per second while it is inside, whatever its mass (`0.0`
/// is none).
type volume = { center: Vec3.t, size: Vec3.t, force: Vec3.t, turbulence: float, drag: float }

/// A persistent force field: a draft through a broken window, an air vent.
///
/// Declare it in `Physics.scene`'s list beside the bodies. Every physics step,
/// each awake dynamic body whose centre of mass is inside gets the volume's
/// force and gusts. A body the draft can't move (a crate under a weak fan)
/// settles and sleeps as usual; declaring, changing or removing a volume
/// wakes the bodies inside it. It is declared each frame like a body and stops when it
/// is no longer declared.
/// The tag names the volume (it seeds the gusts) and is not a body:
/// reads, commands and contacts never see it. For paper that flutters and
/// settles, give the light bodies `Physics.linearDamping` and
/// `Physics.angularDamping` too.
///
/// ```functor
/// Physics.forceVolume(fanTag, { center: Vec3.make(0.0, 1.0, 3.0),
///   size: Vec3.make(2.0, 2.0, 6.0), force: Vec3.make(0.0, 0.0, 2.0),
///   turbulence: 0.5, drag: 0.0 })
/// ```
let forceVolume : (tag, volume) => body

/// Declare a physics world from gravity, bodies and force volumes.
let scene : (Vec3.t, List<body>) => world

/// Build fixed and kinematic bodies once and reuse them while their inputs
/// stay the same — `Scene.memo` for physics.
///
/// `Physics.memo(key, deps, builder)` calls `builder(deps)` only when `key` is
/// new, `deps` changed, or after a hot reload, and returns ONE group for
/// `Physics.scene`'s list. An unchanged group costs the frame a pointer
/// compare: the builder doesn't run and reconcile skips its bodies.
///
/// ```functor
/// let staticBodies = (rev) => List.map(crateBody, staticCrates)
/// let physics = (model) => Physics.scene(gravity, [
///   Physics.memo("statics", model.staticsRev, staticBodies),
///   player(model),
/// ])
/// ```
///
/// - `key`, `deps` and the builder follow `Scene.memo`'s rules: `deps` is
///   plain data (pass a revision number, not `Bytes` or textures), the builder
///   reads only `deps` and top-level definitions (calling an `impure[…]`
///   built-in, such as a world read, is an error), and a key unused for a
///   couple of seconds is forgotten.
/// - Fixed and kinematic bodies only: a dynamic body, a heightfield or a
///   fused room inside is an error. A kinematic body that moves belongs
///   flat, since its deps would change every frame.
/// - Each tag belongs to one place. A flat body reusing a group's tag, two
///   groups sharing a tag, or one key twice in a scene is an error.
///   Reads and commands address group bodies by tag like flat ones.
/// - The group is not a body: `Physics.at` or any attribute on it is an
///   error; apply it inside the builder.
let memo : (string, 'd, ('d) => List<body>) => body

/// Read a body's live, stepped world position.
///
/// Answers with the LAST stepped world, so a pre-step caller (`tick`, the
/// `physics` hook) sees the previous step and `draw` sees this frame's.
impure[physics]
let position : (tag) => position

/// Read a body's live, stepped linear velocity.
///
/// The read counterpart of `Physics.setVelocity`. (`Physics.velocity` is the
/// body-builder attribute that sets an *initial* velocity.)
impure[physics]
let linearVelocity : (tag) => velocity

/// Whether a character's last move ended on walkable ground (`false` before
/// its first move). A world read like `Physics.position`.
impure[physics]
let grounded : (tag) => bool

/// Cast a ray against the world and get the nearest hit immediately.
///
/// Unlike `Physics.raycast` — an effect whose answer arrives through `update`
/// after the step — this answers in place, so `tick` can branch on it while
/// deciding. It reads the world as of the last step, like `Physics.position`:
/// in `tick` that is the previous step, in `draw` this frame's. A miss is
/// `hit: false` with zeroed fields, not an error. `dir` need not be
/// normalized, so `maxDist` is in world units.
impure[physics]
let cast : (Vec3.t, Vec3.t, float) => rayHit

/// `Physics.cast`, ignoring one body — the grounding probe.
///
/// A ray cast from inside a character's own capsule would otherwise hit that
/// capsule at distance 0 and report the character standing on itself. Excluding
/// a tag that isn't in the world excludes nothing.
impure[physics]
let castExcluding : (tag, Vec3.t, Vec3.t, float) => rayHit

/// Sweep a box, sphere or capsule and get the first body it touches.
///
/// The shape moves unrotated from `origin` along `dir`, up to `maxDist`.
/// `distance` is how far it travelled, `x, y, z` the contact point and
/// `nx, ny, nz` the hit surface's outward normal. A shape that starts
/// overlapping something hits it at distance 0 unless it moves out of it.
/// Like `Physics.cast` it answers in place from the last step, and hits both
/// sides of a trimesh face.
impure[physics]
let shapeCast : (shape, Vec3.t, Vec3.t, float) => rayHit

/// `Physics.shapeCast`, ignoring one body: a sweep from a character's own
/// position would otherwise hit its own collider.
impure[physics]
let shapeCastExcluding : (tag, shape, Vec3.t, Vec3.t, float) => rayHit

/// Which bodies `Physics.sweep` can hit; sensors never. `SweepFixed` is the held
/// prop's filter: it stops at the level, not at other props or the hand's
/// own kinematic body. `SweepNotKinematic` adds dynamic bodies; `SweepAll` adds
/// kinematic ones too.
type sweepFilter =
  | SweepFixed
  | SweepNotKinematic
  | SweepAll

/// `Physics.sweep`'s first hit: `fraction` in [0, 1] of the way from
/// `fromPose` to `toPose`, the contact point `x, y, z`, the hit surface's
/// outward normal `nx, ny, nz`, and the body hit.
type sweepHit = {
  fraction: float,
  x: float, y: float, z: float,
  nx: float, ny: float, nz: float,
  tag: tag
}

/// Sweep a box, sphere or capsule from one pose to another, turning as it
/// moves, and get the first body it touches: `Option.None` when the way is
/// clear.
///
/// The poses are `Scene.atPose`'s, so a prop drawn at a pose is swept at
/// exactly that orientation (local `+Z` along `forward`, `+Y` along `up`).
/// The shape moves at a steady speed and turns at a steady rate about its
/// centre, so a long prop rotating into a wall stops there even when its
/// centre hardly moves — where `Physics.shapeCast` would pass.
///
/// A shape that starts overlapping something hits it at fraction 0 when the
/// motion presses deeper, and passes when it moves out, so a held prop
/// pushed into a wall stays there and comes back out freely. With
/// `fromPose == toPose` it answers whether that pose overlaps anything (a
/// hit at fraction 0) or not. Like `Physics.cast` it answers in place from
/// the last step.
///
/// Against a heightfield or the fused room's floor the turn is followed in
/// linear steps that each hold their rotation, to within 5 mm.
///
/// ```functor
/// let blocked = Physics.sweep(Physics.box(0.04, 0.04, 0.8), held, wanted, Physics.SweepFixed)
/// ```
impure[physics]
let sweep : (shape, Camera3D.mappedPose, Camera3D.mappedPose, sweepFilter) => Option.t<sweepHit>

/// Apply a body's live transform to a scene node.
///
/// The scene is last for piping. An unknown tag is an error, except while
/// the world hasn't stepped since a seek, rewind or hot reload (a scrubbed
/// frame from before the body existed): then the node draws translucent at
/// the origin (with a warning).
impure[physics]
let transformed : (tag, Scene.t) => Scene.t

/// Apply an instantaneous impulse to a body.
let applyImpulse : (tag, Vec3.t) => Effect.t
/// Apply a continuous force to a body for the next step.
let applyForce : (tag, Vec3.t) => Effect.t
/// Replace a body's linear velocity — all three axes.
///
/// For a character controller prefer `Physics.setVelocityXZ`: writing the
/// vertical axis every frame fights the solver's own ground contact.
let setVelocity : (tag, Vec3.t) => Effect.t

/// Replace a body's HORIZONTAL velocity, leaving the vertical axis alone.
///
/// The character-controller command. Steering owns x and z while the solver
/// keeps the y it is using to resolve the ground contact, so the game never
/// has to author a vertical velocity it has no opinion about — with
/// `Physics.setVelocity` it must write all three every frame, and the only
/// values available to it are a one-step-stale read or a guess.
///
/// The preserved axis is read from the live world when the command applies —
/// after reconcile, and after any command queued earlier the same frame. So
/// velocity commands in one frame compose as last-write-wins per axis, and an
/// axis nobody wrote is left exactly as the solver left it.
let setVelocityXZ : (tag, float, float) => Effect.t

/// Replace a body's VERTICAL velocity, leaving the horizontal plane alone.
///
/// A jump that keeps the run: unlike `Physics.applyImpulse` the result does
/// not depend on the body's mass, and unlike `Physics.setVelocity` it does not
/// discard the horizontal momentum the character arrived with.
let setVelocityY : (tag, float) => Effect.t
/// Move a body immediately to a world position.
let teleport : (tag, Vec3.t) => Effect.t

/// Walk a `Physics.character` by up to a displacement, in metres, at the next
/// step: velocity × `dt`, including the vertical speed. It collides and slides,
/// steps up ledges up to `stepHeight`, and stays on the ground going down
/// unless the move is upward (a jump). Several moves before one step chain. Like
/// `Physics.cast`, a move sees bodies as of the last step, so one declared
/// this frame doesn't block it yet.
let move : (tag, Vec3.t) => Effect.t

/// How an explosion's push fades from full at its centre to zero at its
/// radius: `Linear` is `1 - d / r`, `Quadratic` is `(1 - d / r)²` (most of
/// the push close in).
type falloff =
  | Linear
  | Quadratic

/// `Physics.explodeWith`'s options. `lift` biases each push upward, as games
/// do: `0.0` is purely radial, `1.0` lifts a level push to 45°.
type blast = { falloff: falloff, lift: float }

/// Push every dynamic body near a point away from it, at the next step:
/// `(center, radius, strength)`.
///
/// A body is in range when its collider comes within `radius` of `center`,
/// and the push lands at that nearest point, so off-centre hits spin it.
/// `strength` is the impulse at the centre, in newton-seconds, so lighter
/// bodies fly further; it fades linearly to zero at `radius`. Sleeping bodies
/// wake. Kinematic and fixed bodies, characters included, are untouched. The
/// blast is a recorded command like `Physics.applyImpulse`, so replays repeat
/// it exactly.
let explode : (Vec3.t, float, float) => Effect.t

/// `Physics.explode` with a falloff and an upward lift:
/// `Physics.explodeWith(center, 4.0, 6.0, { falloff: Physics.Quadratic, lift: 0.5 })`.
let explodeWith : (Vec3.t, float, float, blast) => Effect.t

/// Cast a ray and tag its `Physics.rayHit` result as a message.
let raycast : (Vec3.t, Vec3.t, float, (rayHit) => 'msg) => Effect.t

/// Subscribe to contact begin/end events and tag them as messages.
let events : ((collisionEvent) => 'msg) => Sub.t
